{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Store the baseline in the projection the comparison already computes: `description` as\n`normalize_rich_text(raw)`, `status`/`priority` as `.name`. Expected ~-66% on an **18.3 MB**\n(18,257,550-byte) file. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why this is safe without a comparator change\n`outbound_field_diff.py:187,196` canonicalize BOTH sides at **read** time via\n`inbound_mapper.map_remote_to_local` (docstring `:183`: \"storage stays vendor-shaped, mapped at READ\ntime only\"), and both canonicalizers are idempotent on already-normalized values:\n`normalize_rich_text` (`inbound_fields.py:136-145`) returns `adf_to_text(body)` for dicts and\n`str(body)` otherwise; `_extract_jira_field_value` (`:92-96`) reads `name`/`displayName` for dicts and\npasses strings through. So ADF-dict and plain-text baselines canonicalize identically and a mixed\nstore needs no new comparator. This also makes the change **revert-safe** — a normalized-string\nbaseline round-trips unchanged through pre-A3 code.\n\nCORRECTION carried forward: an earlier framing rested on `_extract_jira_field`/`_diff_fields`, which\nhave **zero production callers**. Validate against the live path only.\n\n## Why assignee is EXCLUDED — data loss, not conflict noise\n`_assignee_candidates` (`outbound_field_diff.py:56-67`) builds its match set from the scalar **plus\nevery non-None identity form**, and `_suppressed_by_inbound` (`:250-256`) routes assignee through\n`_assignee_matches`. Collapsing three identity forms to one string shrinks that candidate set and\nloses inbound-directionality suppression, so local-wins **silently clobbers a concurrent remote\nassignee edit**. (The strict `assignee_identity` equality at `:390-394` is the lesser, secondary\neffect.) Keep assignee raw.\n\n## Trap to pin\nStore exactly `normalize_rich_text(raw)` — any other text extraction (fit/truncate/strip variants)\ndiverges from what the same ADF decodes to on the read path and fabricates a permanent diff.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A **dict-shaped** baseline fed through the live `compute_update_fields` path yields the **same\n      verdict** as its normalized-string equivalent. Tier: **integration** through the real\n      production comparison path. **This test does not exist today** —\n      `test_binding_baseline.py:36-59` and `test_baseline_arbitration.py:91` use flat strings only, so\n      a no-op-on-strings normalizer would leave them green while proving nothing. This is the RED case.\n- [ ] The direction-preservation suite, extended with assignee shape cases (email / accountId /\n      displayName), passes unchanged — proving assignee arbitration is untouched. Tier: **property/unit**.\n- [ ] Baseline entries for `description`/`status`/`priority` are scalars after one pass; `assignee`\n      remains a dict. Tier: **integration** asserting stored shapes.\n- [ ] A corpus diff over real bindings shows **zero mutation delta** before vs after normalization.\n      Tier: **repo-level corpus script**.\n- [ ] The same corpus, after normalization, produces **zero mutation delta** when evaluated by the\n      pre-A3 live comparison code versus the pre-normalization corpus. This is the reverse-direction\n      rollback oracle: normalized strings remain behaviorally compatible with the old reader.\n      Tier: **repo-level corpus script using both revisions**.\n- [ ] `bindings.json` size drops by >=50%. Verify: byte comparison before/after one full pass.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py`,\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py`,\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_baseline.py`,\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_baseline_arbitration.py`,\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_baseline_normalization_heldout.py`,\n`tests/integration/rebar_reconciler/test_baseline_normalization.py`,\n`scripts/measure_baseline_normalization.py`, and\n`docs/experiments/bridge-state-baseline-normalization.md`\n\nScope: S-M. Depends on A2-1."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785783363925389001,"uuid":"79c9db4e-f048-4216-814b-4d8a759d268e"}